## Commands

| Task          | Command          |
| ------------- | ---------------- |
| Install       | `pnpm install`   |
| Run (dev)     | `pnpm dev`       |
| Lint          | `pnpm lint`      |
| Type check    | `pnpm typecheck` |
| Build         | `pnpm build`     |
| Run the build | `pnpm start`     |
| Write the migrations | `pnpm db:generate` |
| Apply the migrations | `pnpm db:migrate`  |
| Add the seed records | `pnpm db:seed`     |

## Database

The app uses Postgres with Drizzle ORM. Copy `.env.example` to
`.env.local`, and set `DATABASE_URL`. The schema is in
`src/shared/db/schema/`. `pnpm db:generate` writes the SQL migrations
into `drizzle/`: keep them in version control. With
`DATABASE_MIGRATE=true`, the server applies them when it starts.

### Seed data

`pnpm db:seed` fills the tables of `DATABASE_URL` with seed records,
for example `DATABASE_URL=pglite:./.pglite pnpm db:seed`. The records
come from the app definition, and are the same on each run. Apply the
migrations first. The seed adds each record of the `seed` section of
the definition that the database does not have (by ID), and does not
change a record that it has. It adds the generated mock records only
if all tables are empty. With `DATABASE_SEED=true`, the server
runs the seed when it starts, after the migrations.
`src/shared/db/seed-data.ts` holds the records.

### Local database

To run the app without a Postgres server, use PGlite, an embedded
Postgres that runs in the Node.js process. Set these values in
`.env.local`, then run `pnpm db:generate` and `pnpm dev`:

```sh
DATABASE_URL=pglite:./.pglite
DATABASE_MIGRATE=true
```

PGlite keeps the data in the folder `.pglite/`. It uses the same schema
and the same migrations as Postgres. Only one process can open the
folder: stop the server before you run `pnpm db:migrate`. Use Postgres
in production.

## Docker

`docker compose up --build` starts Postgres and the app on
<http://localhost:3000>. The app applies the migrations and adds the
seed records when it starts.
The `Dockerfile` builds a standalone Next.js server that runs as a
non-root user. If `drizzle/` does not exist, the build writes the first
migration from the schema. `node migrate.mjs` in the image applies the
migrations and stops.

## Production

{% set name = app.name ~ "-web" %}
- Health: `/health` gives `{"status":"ok"}` while the database answers,
  else 503. The probes of the deployment files use it.
- Rate limits: `RATE_LIMIT_PER_MINUTE` sets the number of requests that
  each client address can send in each minute (0, the default, sets no
  limit). `proxy.ts` counts the pages, the server actions, and the route
  handlers, but not the static files and `/health`. Over the limit, the
  app gives 429 with `Retry-After`. A page view makes 10 to 25 requests
  (the page, the prefetches of its links, and the data of its views), so
  the deployment files set 1200, and Compose sets no limit.
{% if nextjs.redis %}
  The counts are in the memory of each process, or in Redis with
  `RATE_LIMIT_STORE_URL` (for example `redis://localhost:6379/0`): then
  all replicas share one limit. When Redis is not available, the app
  logs a warning and lets the requests through, or gives 503 with
  `RATE_LIMIT_FAIL_CLOSED=true`. Compose starts Redis with
  `--profile redis`.
{% else %}
  The counts are in the memory of each process, so each replica has its
  own limit.
{% endif %}
- The client address: the app counts by the address that the proxy in
  front of the app (an ingress, a load balancer, or Cloud Run) adds to
  the end of `X-Forwarded-For`. `RATE_LIMIT_TRUSTED_HOPS` (default 1)
  is the number of addresses that your proxies add: set 2 behind a load
  balancer of Google Cloud, which adds the address of the client and its
  own address. Clients must reach the app only through the proxy, else
  a client can send any `X-Forwarded-For`.
{% if "kubernetes" in nextjs.deploy %}
- Kubernetes: `deploy/kubernetes/` has a Kustomize folder with a
  Deployment (2 replicas, a non-root user, a read-only file system), a
  Service, and a Job that applies the migrations. The database is
  outside the cluster. Push the image, set its name in
  `kustomization.yaml`, copy `secret.example.env` to `secret.env`, and
  set the values. Then run
  `kubectl delete job {{ name }}-migrate --ignore-not-found` and
  `kubectl apply -k deploy/kubernetes`.
{% endif %}
{% if "knative" in nextjs.deploy %}
{% set image = "registry.example.com/" ~ name ~ ":0.1.0" %}
{# The secrets of deploy/knative/service.yaml: each one is a secret of its own. #}
{% set secrets = ["DATABASE_URL"] + (["RATE_LIMIT_STORE_URL"] if nextjs.redis else [])
    + (["AUTH_SECRET"] if definition.auth else [])
    + ((definition.auth.providers.values() | map(attribute="client_secret_env") | list) if definition.auth else [])
    + (ts_apis.values() | selectattr("secret_env") | map(attribute="secret_env") | list) %}
- Knative and Google Cloud Run: `deploy/knative/service.yaml` is a
  Knative Service (0 to 10 instances, the probes, and the resource
  limits), and Cloud Run accepts the same file. Push the image, and set
  its name in `service.yaml` and `migrate-job.yaml`. Each secret is a
  secret of its own, with the key (Knative) or the version (Cloud Run)
  `latest`. Apply the migrations before each deployment of a new image.
  On a cluster with Knative Serving:

  ```sh
{% for env in secrets %}
  kubectl create secret generic {{ name }}-{{ env | lower | replace("_", "-") }} --from-literal=latest='<{{ env }}>'
{% endfor %}
  kubectl delete job {{ name }}-migrate --ignore-not-found
  kubectl apply -f deploy/knative/migrate-job.yaml
  kubectl wait --for=condition=complete job/{{ name }}-migrate --timeout=10m
  kubectl apply -f deploy/knative/service.yaml
  ```

  On Cloud Run, a Cloud Run job applies the migrations. The database
  must accept connections from Cloud Run, and the service account of the
  service and of the job must have access to the secrets:

  ```sh
{% for env in secrets %}
  printf %s '<{{ env }}>' | gcloud secrets create {{ name }}-{{ env | lower | replace("_", "-") }} --data-file=-
{% endfor %}
  gcloud run jobs deploy {{ name }}-migrate --image {{ image }} \
    --command node --args migrate.mjs \
    --set-secrets DATABASE_URL={{ name }}-database-url:latest --execute-now --wait
  gcloud run services replace deploy/knative/service.yaml
  ```

  A new Cloud Run service accepts only requests with a Google sign-in.
  For a public app, run `gcloud run services add-iam-policy-binding
  {{ name }} --member=allUsers --role=roles/run.invoker`.
